<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of the Tooth</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sri Dalada Maligawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relic-of-the-tooth-of-the-buddha">Sacred Tooth Relic</a>, or Sri Dalada Maligawa, is a Buddhist temple in the royal palace complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, that houses the relic of the Buddha's tooth. Possession of the relic was long seen as conferring the right to rule the island, and the kings of Kandy built the present temple in the 17th and 18th centuries. The annual Esala Perahera procession honours it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to legend, the tooth relic was kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> after the Buddha's death, and when that kingdom was attacked, Princess Hemamali and her brother Prince Dantha brought it to the island on the instructions of their father, King Guhasiva. Hemamali is said to have hidden it in her hair. They landed during the reign of Sirimeghavanna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> (301 to 328), and the king enshrined it at the Meghagiri Vihara. Guarding the relic became the monarch's duty and the custodianship came to symbolise the right to rule the island, so kings built tooth temples close to their palaces at Anuradhapura, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a> and Dambadeniya. Under the Kingdom of Gampola the relic was kept at Niyamgampaya Vihara.</p><p>During the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmapala-of-kotte">Dharmapala of Kotte</a> the relic was hidden in a grinding stone at Delgamuwa Vihara in Ratnapura, and it was later brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vimaladharmasuriya-i">Vimaladharmasuriya I</a> built a two-storey building for it. In 1603, when the Portuguese invaded Kandy, it was carried to Meda Mahanuwara in Dumbara, and it was recovered under Rajasinha II. The present temple was built by Vira Narendra Sinha, and the octagonal Paththirippuwa and the moat were added under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-vikrama-rajasinha">Sri Vikrama Rajasinha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Sri Dalada Maligawa stands in the royal palace complex of the former Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> and is a World Heritage Site. Monks of the Malwathu and Asgiri chapters conduct worship in the inner chamber three times daily, at dawn, at noon and in the evening. On Wednesdays the relic is bathed symbolically with a herbal preparation, and the water is distributed among those present. The relic is encased in seven golden caskets inlaid with gems, which represent a stupa. The golden canopy and fence were built in 1987.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The annual Kandy Esala Perahera pays homage to the relic. Beside the temple stands the royal palace and the audience hall, built in 1783, where the Kandyan Convention was read out on 2 March 1815. The temple was attacked in 1989 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janatha-vimukthi-peramuna">Janatha Vimukthi Peramuna</a>, which meant to capture the relic, and in 1998 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-tigers-of-tamil-eelam">Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam</a>, whose bomb destroyed the main entrance gate and damaged the palace front. A public exposition in April 2025 drew about 450,000 devotees in one day and caused four deaths from heat exhaustion and fatigue.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Temple of the Tooth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings guarded the Tooth Relic in the <b>Temple of the Tooth</b> at Kandy, the symbol of Sinhalese kingship.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kandy">Kingdom of Kandy</a></li></ul></section>
